Subject: [PATCH] fix CGP-qjm4-c96g-8hhw: reject unsigned SAML assertions by
 default

This addresses CWE-345 (insufficient verification of data
authenticity) in two classes of the affected artifact,
cxf-rt-rs-security-oauth2-saml:
org.apache.cxf.rs.security.oauth2.grants.saml.Saml2BearerGrantHandler.
validateToken and org.apache.cxf.rs.security.saml.AbstractSamlInHandler.
validateToken. Both methods handled an unsigned SAML assertion in an
else branch that performed exactly one check: whether the transport
session presented any TLS client certificate at all
(getTLSCertificates(message) == null). If some certificate accepted by
the container truststore was present, the unsigned assertion was
accepted and used to issue an OAuth2 access token, or to install a
SecurityContext, for whatever subject the assertion's own Issuer and
Subject named.

The root cause is that presence of a TLS client certificate is a
channel property, not proof of the peer's claimed identity, and the
code never compared the TLS client identity to the assertion's Issuer
or Subject. Because an unsigned assertion is authored in its entirety
by the sender, every identity-bearing field in it (Issuer, Subject
NameID, and any SubjectConfirmation or SubjectKeyInfo) is
attacker-controlled; an attacker holding any accepted client
certificate could name an arbitrary, including privileged, subject and
be authenticated as that subject. This held for every
SubjectConfirmation method the assertion declared -- Bearer,
Sender-Vouches, Holder-of-Key, an unrecognized or custom method, or
none at all -- since the unbound accept path did not depend on which
method was used.

The fix introduces a single shared chokepoint,
org.apache.cxf.rs.security.saml.SamlTlsIdentityBinding, and wires it
into the unsigned-assertion branch of both validateToken methods. Its
isUnsignedAssertionTlsBound check unconditionally returns false: no
comparison between the assertion's own key material and the TLS peer
certificate is trusted to prove identity, because an attacker
controls both sides of any such comparison. An unsigned assertion is
therefore rejected by default in both sinks regardless of
confirmation method, and identity can only be established by a
signature that chains to the configured sigVerCrypto trust anchor (the
pre-existing signed-assertion path, left unchanged). A
deployment-scoped opt-out, SamlTlsIdentityBinding.
isUnboundAcceptanceAllowed (system property
org.apache.cxf.saml.allow-unbound-unsigned-assertion), lets an
operator explicitly re-enable the legacy presence-only behavior; it
defaults off, is read through a single strict true/false parser where
only "1"/"true"/"yes"/"on" engage it, and logs a WARN naming the
CVE/CWE on every use.

The regression test class
Saml2UnsignedAssertionTlsBindingExploitTest (with its
UnsignedSamlCallbackHandler helper) asserts that an unsigned assertion
presenting an accepted-but-unrelated TLS client certificate is
rejected by Saml2BearerGrantHandler.createAccessToken and by a real
AbstractSamlInHandler subclass, across Bearer, Sender-Vouches,
Holder-of-Key, an unrecognized confirmation method, no
SubjectConfirmation element at all, and a dual Bearer plus
Holder-of-Key SubjectConfirmation on one assertion. It also asserts
the escape hatch fails closed for every non-affirmative value and
engages only on an explicit affirmative, and positively confirms the
preserved, legitimate behavior: a genuinely signed assertion is still
accepted through AbstractSamlInHandler.

